/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Oil in a loaded journal bearing is dragged through a gap that narrows in the direction of rotation, and the Reynolds equation gives the pressure in that film.
   - The input is the Sommerfeld number of Chapter 5.4, S = (μn/p)(r/c)², with μ in Pa·s at the film temperature, n in rev/s, p = W/(LD) in Pa and r/c the clearance ratio.
   - Read the columns like this. ε is the eccentricity ratio and h₀/c = 1 − ε the film thickness ratio. φ is the attitude angle between the load line and the line of centres.
   - The curves assume a steady load, a Newtonian oil of one viscosity, no misalignment and no oil groove inside the loaded arc.
   - The heat balance assumes all the friction heat leaves in the oil flow: Pf = ρ cp Q ΔT.
   - ΔT is the oil temperature rise in °C, p the bearing pressure in MPa, (r/c)f and Q/(rcnL) the chart readings.
   - The charts were drawn for one viscosity, yet the oil that carries the load also heats up, and hot oil is thinner.
   - Two checks close the design.
*/
