/* book Worked example: Q: The bearing of Chapter 5.4 has D = L = 50 mm, c = 0.05 mm, W = 10 kN, n = 25 rev/s and μ = 0.02 Pa·s, giving S = 0.03125. Using the L/D = 1 table, find ε, h₀, the friction coefficient, the friction power, the oil flow and the temperature rise. Is the film thick enough?
   1. S = 0.03125 lies between the rows S = 0.0446 (ε = 0.8) and S = 0.0188 (ε = 0.9). Fraction of the way: t = (0.0446 − 0.03125)/(0.0446 − 0.0188) = 0.01335/0.0258 = 0.517.
   2. ε = 0.8 + 0.517 × 0.1 = 0.852, so h₀/c = 1 − 0.852 = 0.148 and h₀ = 0.148 × 0.05 = 0.0074 mm = 7.4 μm.
   3. Friction variable: (r/c)f = 1.70 − 0.517 × (1.70 − 1.05) = 1.364. With r/c = 25/0.05 = 500, f = 1.364/500 = 0.00273.
   4. Torque T = f W r = 0.00273 × 10 000 × 0.025 = 0.682 N·m. Power Pf = 2πnT = 2π × 25 × 0.682 = 107.1 W.
   5. Flow variable: 4.62 + 0.517 × (4.74 − 4.62) = 4.682. Q = 4.682 × r c n L = 4.682 × 25 × 0.05 × 25 × 50 = 7316 mm³/s = 7.32 cm³/s.
   6. Temperature rise: p = 4 MPa, so ΔT = 8.30 × 4 × 1.364/4.682 = 9.7 °C. Check by energy: 107.1 W ÷ (860 × 1760 × 7.316 × 10⁻⁶ m³/s) = 9.7 °C.
   7. Film check: Trumpler asks for h₀ ≥ 0.005 + 0.00004 × 50 = 0.007 mm. The bearing gives 0.0074 mm, a pass with almost no margin. A thicker oil or a longer bearing raises S and the film.
   Answer: ε ≈ 0.85, h₀ ≈ 7.4 μm, f ≈ 0.0027, friction power ≈ 107 W, Q ≈ 7.3 cm³/s and ΔT ≈ 9.7 °C. The film only just meets the 7 μm guide, so the design needs a thicker oil or a longer bearing. */
