/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A ball or roller bearing rarely snaps.
   - Both come from the catalogue, not from derivation.
   - A real bearing carries radial load Fr, in N, perpendicular to the shaft axis, and axial or thrust load Fa, in N, along the axis.
   - P in N; Fr radial load in N; Fa axial load in N; X radial factor, Y thrust factor, from the catalogue for that bearing.
   - P is the pure radial load giving the same fatigue as the actual mix.
   - Single-row deep-groove ball bearings take small thrust.
   - L10 is rating life in million revolutions; C and P are in the same unit (N or kN); p = 3 for ball bearings, p = 10/3 for roller bearings.
   - The subscript 10 is a probability, not a count.
   - L10h = 10⁶ (C/P)p ÷ (60 n)
   - L10h is the life in hours; n is the speed in rpm; 10⁶ turns million revolutions into revolutions and 60 n is the revolutions per hour.
*/
