/* book exam traps: Mixing newtons and kilonewtons inside C/P. Convert C and P to the same unit before dividing. | Using p = 3 for a roller bearing. Rollers take p = 10/3, and the same C/P then gives a longer life. | Putting Fr in place of P when the bearing also carries thrust. If Fa/Fr > e, P > Fr and life drops. | Dropping the 10⁶ when converting L10 to L10h; the answer becomes a million times too small. | Reading L10 as the life every bearing reaches or as 10% reliability. It is the 90% reliability life. | Using C₀ in the life formula. C₀ guards against permanent denting at rest; only C enters L10. */
